{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "You now know **why** arrays exist and **how** to use them. This chapter is about **when**: recognising, from a problem statement, that an array is the right tool — and noticing when it isn't."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "What arrays are great at"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Reading and writing by position** in O(1): \"the 5th element\", \"the value at index i\", \"the cell at row r, column c\".",
        "**Walking through everything in order**, which is also very fast in practice: the elements sit side by side in memory, so the CPU's cache loads them in batches.",
        "**Storing data compactly**: no pointers or extra bookkeeping per element.",
        "**Adding and removing at the end** in O(1) (for growable arrays)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Signals in a problem statement"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "These words and shapes almost always mean the problem is an array problem — and hint at the technique:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**\"index\", \"position\", \"i-th\", \"k-th\"** — you'll access elements by position.",
        "**\"subarray\", \"contiguous\", \"window\", \"consecutive days\"** — a slice of neighbouring elements: think sliding window, prefix sums, or Kadane's algorithm.",
        "**\"sorted array\"** — two pointers or binary search are nearby.",
        "**\"in place\", \"O(1) extra space\"** — rearrange the array itself, usually with read/write or two-ends pointers.",
        "**\"grid\", \"matrix\", \"board\", \"image\"** — a 2D array.",
        "**Values in a small range** (\"lowercase letters\", \"numbers from 1 to n\", \"0, 1 and 2\") — an array can act as a counter indexed by value."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "When an array is NOT the best tool"
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "src": "/drawings/d5e226cb-6ed1-47a3-82ca-d954ef7c57a7.svg",
      "alt": "A decision guide. If you need fast lookup by value, use a hash map or set. If you insert or delete in the middle often, consider a linked list or deque. If you always need the smallest or largest item, use a heap. If you access by position or walk in order, use an array.",
      "width": 782,
      "height": 558,
