    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The costs behind that guide:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Search by value**: array O(n) (O(log n) if sorted) — hash set **O(1)** on average.",
        "**Insert/delete in the middle**: array O(n) because of shifting — linked list **O(1)** once you're at the spot.",
        "**Get the minimum repeatedly while adding items**: array O(n) each time — heap **O(log n)**.",
        "**Access the i-th element**: array **O(1)** — linked list O(n), because it must walk from the head."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In practice, the answer is often **\"an array, plus something\"**: an array plus a hash map (Two Sum), an array plus a heap (k largest elements), an array used as a stack. Stacks, queues, heaps and hash tables are themselves usually built on arrays underneath."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The array as a counter or lookup table"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "One of the most useful \"when\" moments: if the possible values are small whole numbers, you don't need a hash map at all. An array indexed by the value is faster and simpler."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "Counting letters in a word: `counts = new Array(26)`, indexed by `letter - 'a'`.",
        "Checking which numbers from 1 to n appear: a boolean array of size n + 1.",
        "Sorting values known to be 0, 1 or 2: count them — or use the Dutch national flag in one pass."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "A quick self-test"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "For each description, which would you reach for? (Answers below.)"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "A) \"Return the k-th element of this list\" — a hundred million times.",
        "B) \"Has this user ID appeared before?\" — for a stream of IDs.",
        "C) \"The longest run of consecutive days where the temperature rose.\"",
        "D) \"Repeatedly take the cheapest task and add new tasks as they arrive.\""
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Answers:** A) an array — O(1) access by position. B) a hash set — O(1) lookup by value. C) an array walked once, carrying the current run length. D) a heap — though it's stored in an array under the hood."
    }
